        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          The acitve dosimetry system was composed of tissue-equivalent ionization chambers, the response characterisitcs of which for all ionizing radiation match closely the response characteristics of soft muscle.  
Two types of active dosimeters were used for data collection during the Gemini 4 and 6 missions.   One type was designed to be equipped with a fixed sensor (type I), the other type involved a moveable sensor (type V).  The Gemini 6 instrumentation differed slightly, due to the inclusion of a 2.5 g/cm2 depth dose shield for the fixed dosimeter.
The sensor subassembly was made up of a tissue equivalent gas cavity surrounded by a conducting spherical tissue equivalent plastic wall, an internal radiation source, and a tetrode electrometer vacuum-tube preamplifier operated as a floating grid logarithmic triode.
All instruments were powered by regulated voltage from the spacecraft, and the output signal from each instrument was recorded continuously throughout each flight.  Each instrument was capable of recording instantaneous dose rates ranging between 0.1 millirad per hour to 100 rads per hour.
        </div>

